WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.575
A concurrent data structure needs a correctness contract before it needs a clever implementation.

2
00:00:04.575 --> 00:00:12.419
The contract answers simple questions that become urgent under load: who owns the structure, which threads may read it, which threads may modify it,

3
00:00:12.419 --> 00:00:16.341
and what a caller is allowed to keep after an operation returns.

4
00:00:16.341 --> 00:00:20.263
If the contract is vague, each caller fills in the blanks differently.

5
00:00:20.263 --> 00:00:22.877
One caller may assume a reference remains valid.

6
00:00:22.877 --> 00:00:25.818
Another may assume a lookup and erase cannot interleave.

7
00:00:25.818 --> 00:00:31.047
A third may combine operations that were only designed to be safe one at a time.

8
00:00:31.047 --> 00:00:32.681
Ownership boundaries are especially important.

9
00:00:32.681 --> 00:00:35.296
Sometimes a structure owns every object it stores.

10
00:00:35.296 --> 00:00:37.584
Sometimes it only indexes objects owned elsewhere.

11
00:00:37.584 --> 00:00:40.852
Sometimes it returns shared ownership, snapshots, or short-lived views.

12
00:00:40.852 --> 00:00:45.754
Each model can be valid, but it must be documented and enforced by the API.

13
00:00:45.754 --> 00:00:50.656
A design that returns a pointer to internal state also transfers responsibility to the caller.

14
00:00:50.656 --> 00:00:58.500
That may be acceptable only when the caller receives a scoped guard, a shared ownership handle, or another mechanism that makes the lifetime rule

15
00:00:58.500 --> 00:00:58.827
visible.

16
00:00:58.827 --> 00:01:01.114
Invariants often span more than one field.

17
00:01:01.114 --> 00:01:10.265
A container may be paired with a size counter, a map may be paired with a reverse index, and a cache may be paired with timestamps and eviction

18
00:01:10.265 --> 00:01:10.592
metadata.

19
00:01:10.592 --> 00:01:15.167
Protecting only the obvious container is not enough if related fields can change separately.

20
00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:19.743
The contract should identify which lock, ownership rule, or synchronization boundary protects each invariant.

21
00:01:19.743 --> 00:01:23.338
Good tests and code review then have something concrete to verify.

22
00:01:23.338 --> 00:01:28.240
Defensive design turns concurrency from a set of private assumptions into a shared engineering agreement.
